  "query_preview": "..Synoptic pattern evolving more or less as expected. A \nbroad upper low is extended southward from the PacNW, with an \nassociated shortwave impulse pivoting through nearby and a cold \nfrontal boundary advancing into northwest Utah. A stream of \ntropical moisture has continued to advect into the region within \nthe enhanced southwest flow, resulting in a somewhat narrow band \nof persistent shower activity. A lack of destabilization has \nlargely kept these showers shallow and more stratiform in nature, \nand observed rain rates have struggled to push much past 0.10\" per\nhour or so, even with PWATs around 0.8\" to 1.2\". A few slightly \nmore organized cells managed to develop across northwest Utah and \nproduce some instances of stronger wind gusts, but otherwise this \nsystem has not produced much in the way of overly impactful \nweather locally.\n\nThe cold front will continue to progress southeastward through \nthe night, with lingering moisture helping to maintain some \nscattered shower activity across northern Utah into Monday \nmorning. The more marked moisture plume will shift eastward \nthough, allowing precipitation to largely diminish elsewhere \nacross the forecast area through the overnight hours. The front \nwill progress into central Utah before eventually stalling and \nbeginning to wash out, but cooler temperatures will move in behind\nit. Across northern Utah and southwest Wyoming, afternoon highs \nwill run around 5-10 degrees below climatological normal. Across \nsouthern Utah temperatures will increase in comparison to Sunday \ngiven more limited cloud cover, but even then highs will be near \nto a little below normal.\n\nMoving through the work week, models favor a largely dry and \nzonal flow being maintained overhead. Temperatures will gradually \nmoderate back upwards in turn. In comparison to prior forecast \ncycles, ensembles seem to be trending away from much of a moisture\nreturn, and as such precipitation chances remain low. There is \nsome semblance of another northern stream system digging into the \narea around the weekend, but minimal consensus on the \ndepth/strength of any such feature, or what sort of moisture would\nbe available to interact with. Overall confidence in specifics \nremains low though, so will need to continue to monitor trends.",
